Yes, additions.ndjson can technically be forwarded to Splunk, but it would be only a partial historical feed—not a sufficient telemetry solution.
It can provide:
software_added
release_added
Vendor, artifact/version, commit time and initial security state
It cannot provide:
Later active/inactive/suppressed transitions—those are in scans.ndjson, not additions.ndjson
Libraries under vendor-catalog/library
Removals, sunset transitions, suppression renewal/expiration, or detailed failure reasons
Request initiation, approval/rejection, scan-start, workflow failure or time-to-approval events
Accurate current whitelist totals, because an originally added image may subsequently become inactive

Two important corrections:
metrics-catalog.yml does modify the catalog repository’s generated metrics/ files and .gitignore, although it does not modify vendor-catalog/.
Its push: main trigger applies to this automation repository, not automatically to pushes on RECORE.third-party-catalog. Catalog-only changes are generally picked up by the daily schedule.
Also, forwarding the complete regenerated additions.ndjson every day would create duplicate Splunk events unless the forwarder implements checkpoints or deterministic deduplication.

Recommendation: use additions.ndjson only for a one-time historical backfill or supplementary “artifact added” reporting. It should not be the primary feed for the full telemetry acceptance criteria. 
If this extraction approach is retained, it would at minimum need both additions and scans, library support, additional transition types, and incremental delivery.
